<h2 id='who-its-for'>🎯 Who Esignly Is — and Isn't — For</h2>

<p><strong>Esignly is the right call for:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Independent luxury consignment boutiques</strong> processing 20-200 agreements per month who need legally compliant e-signatures without DocuSign's per-envelope billing model eating margin.</li>
  <li><strong>Jewelry appraisal studios</strong> looking to eliminate the print-sign-scan-email loop and deliver same-day signed appraisal certificates to clients.</li>
  <li><strong>Multi-door luxury retailers</strong> standardizing agreement templates across locations with a shared Business plan library.</li>
  <li><strong>Private client managers</strong> who need to send branded, professional signing experiences that match the white-glove standard their clientele expects.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Esignly is not the right call for:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Operations that need a fully integrated consignment management platform — Esignly handles signatures, not inventory, valuations, or commission accounting. Pair it with a dedicated consignment tool for full coverage.</li>
  <li>Retailers requiring in-person tablet-based signing stations at the point of sale as a first-class feature. The workflow requires adaptation rather than purpose-built kiosk mode.</li>
  <li>Enterprise luxury groups with complex SSO, SAML, or compliance requirements who need a dedicated CSM from day one — the Enterprise tier addresses this but requires a custom negotiation.</li>
</ul>

<div class='callout-blue'>
  <strong>Tip:</strong> If your operation relies heavily on in-person counter transactions where clients sign on a shared device, pair Esignly with a simple iPad kiosk app that opens the signing URL in a locked browser session. It's a 20-minute setup that replicates true kiosk mode without waiting for a native feature build.
</div>

